verdict.reason:
The mathematics is right, and the repair is closed form.
- The transverse section of the normal rack is the normal rack stretched by 1/cos β along the pitch line (x_n = x_t cos β, depth unchanged). A normal circular round therefore becomes an ellipse with semi-axes ρ_n/cos β (along the pitch line) and ρ_n (in depth). The code uses a circle of radius ρ_n/cos β (tooth.rs:918).
- Parameterising the corner by its normal angle φ turns the equation of meshing into a linear equation in the travel: s(φ) = −q_x − (b_c − q_y)·n_x/n_y. No solve is needed for either shape.
- The same parameterisation picks the active arc whatever the sign of b_c.

One imprecision: the gear point is Q(φ) translated by s along the rolling line and then rotated by −(s − a_c)/r, not Q(φ) rotated alone.

The effort is M–L, not M. Everything parameterised by s has to move with it:
- solve_junction, check_severed, trochoid_theta_slope;
- strength.rs:300-346 (numerically unaffected, because rating is on the β=0 virtual spur);
- gear.rs:413/2513;
- verify's cutter_sdf and fillet_envelope_error.

Three closed forms must also change consistently:
- Rack::undercut_shift: run-out ρ_n(1 − sin α_n) instead of ρ_t(1 − sin α_t);
- the fit cap: the normal-plane fit, with w_tip,n = w_tip,t cos β;
- a_c's corner term: ρ_n/(cos α_n cos β).

Two parts hold:
- Production reaches ShaperCut only through Ring (ring.rs:408, kind Internal).
- The External branch is exercised only by shaper.rs's own tests, through equivalent_to_rack, which is a pub function with no production caller.

'Fully built' is overstated. ShaperCut supplies only the fillet curve for an external workpiece: corner_centre_at, trochoid_at, point and tangent, curvature. A shaper-cut external tooth would also need:
- a junction with the involute;
- an undercut or severing criterion for a pinion cutter;
- a root and outline assembly, and DXF;
- a strength seam (ToothOutline);
- metrology.

Ring has all of these, for the internal case only. So effort L is right, and the benefit is real (shouldered and cluster gears).

The docs already say external gears are rack-cut (corrections.md:440). Only shaper.rs's header table (lines 13-17) presents 'external gear, shaper' as a case. Finding lens-unification #2 already reports the missing shaper-cut external gear, with the same evidence.

claim:
The panel decides several engineering facts itself:
- a direction is locked when efficiency ≤ 0 (lockedWays; the comment calls it 'the mirror of Directional::locked');
- a direction never locks when the threshold ≤ 0 (locksAt);
- a warning when a contact ratio is < 1 (the core already raises mesh.contact_ratio_below_one);
- a mesh is idle when power_through is exactly 0, while the core's flow calls it idle at |p| < 1e-9, so the Centres list and the Flow list can disagree;
- a mesh on a distance is internal when exactly one member has a cutter (internalOn), though the core knows each mesh's kind;
- shafts are crossed exactly when angle !== 0;
- a basis is weak when it is 'estimated' or 'chart'.
Each is a second copy of a core rule that no test checks.

proposal:
Put the verdicts on the wire:
- Directional gains `locked: {forward: bool, backward: bool}`;
- LockingFriction gains a `never` state rather than a sign;
- MeshReport gains `internal: bool` and `contact_ok: bool`, or the panel keys its warn class off the presence of the mesh's note;
- MeshCase gains `idle: bool`, taken from groupings' one rule;
- DistanceReport gains `crossed: bool`;
- Basis gains `weak()`, crossed as a flag.
The panel then only reads flags.

