The reference doc cites it as the independent check of hula tooth clearance. It has no unit test. Its expectation that the control touches and does not foul is written only into the golden text, and its tolerances (1e-5, 10 × TOL, 3600 buckets, ±30-bucket search, a 1 mm filter band, PER_ARC = 64) are unexamined. CLAUDE.md gives the reason verify.rs sits in the library ('so the CLI can sweep it'). The same reason applies here, and this one sits in the harness instead.

The CLI computes these itself:
- η₀ as the product of forward mesh efficiencies, written three times (SetView, HulaView, epicyclic_shifts);
- the set 'residual', written two ways: |(n0 + c) − (n1 − c)| and |n0 − n1|;
- the hula ratio products z2·z4 and z2·z4 − z1·z3, from its own HULA_ORDER;
- the worm's d1 = starts.max(1) / sin γ, which assumes m_n = 1, and whose .max(1) is unreachable after Screw::new refuses starts = 0;
- the worm's wheel torque as T·i·η at a hard-coded mu 0.06 with hard-coded materials;
- sliding speed at a literal 3000 rpm as v_s·ω·r, where MeshCase::sliding_velocity already exists;
- three different shaper-sizing rules: ring−5 floored at 4 (hula), cutter + 2 < n (hulaband), and z_ring−5 floored at 6 (meshsweep).

